### Software on the GPUs you already have (the counterfactual that decides everything)

The cheapest accelerator is a better runtime. With the same physics as MDA-1 (same HBM efficiency,
DRAM latency and collective speed-of-light), a dataflow-style program on 4 x GB300 projects
**{{ C.gpu_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user**, {{ K.sw_vs_meas|x }} the measured engine. MDA-1 is
{{ K.MC.vs_sw.p5|x(2) }}-{{ K.MC.vs_sw.p95|x(2) }} of that across the joint Monte Carlo. The software
needs no NRE and no new hardware. It does need engineering: one persistent program per model,
flag-based hand-offs, and TMA L2 prefetch of the next layers' dense weights while experts stream.
Nobody has shown this for a 43-block MoE across 4 GPUs. It is the first thing we would build.

### FPGA (AMD Alveo V80 / Versal HBM class)

* **Capacity forces 16 cards.** 32 GB HBM2e at 819 GB/s per card; ~300 GB of weights needs ≥10 cards (16 for power-of-two sharding). The aggregate bandwidth, 13 TB/s, is below 4 x GB300's 32 TB/s.
* **Compute is thin.** The DSP58 fabric gives ~35 TFLOP/s-equivalent of FP8 per card, so the drafter and 6-row verify GEMVs become partly compute-bound ({{ C.fpga.breakdown_ms.compute|n(2) }} ms of compute on the critical path).
* **Result: {{ C.fpga.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user, below the GPU baseline.**
* **What FPGA is good for here:** a zero-NRE prototype of the *control* ideas (static schedule, hardware router, prefetch, collectives) to de-risk an ASIC. Not a deployment vehicle for a 550B model.

### SRAM-only dataflow (Groq-class) and wafer-scale (Cerebras-class)

* **Capacity sets chip count, not bandwidth.** {{ T.resident_weight_gb|n(0) }} GB of FP4/FP8 weights needs {{ T.rows[4].chips|n }} chips of 230 MB or {{ T.rows[5].chips }} wafers of 44 GB per model replica, at {{ T.rows[4].kw|n(0) }} kW and {{ T.rows[5].kw|n(0) }} kW.
* **Batch-1 speed can be high** in an idealized model ({{ T.rows[4].tok_s_user|n }} and {{ T.rows[5].tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user), because SRAM bandwidth is effectively unlimited. But energy per token at low batch is {{ (C.gpu_tp4.tok_per_joule / T.rows[5].tok_per_joule)|n(0) }}-{{ (C.gpu_tp4.tok_per_joule / T.rows[4].tok_per_joule)|n(0) }}x worse than today's GPUs. These systems recover only by pipelining hundreds of concurrent requests.
* MoE makes this worse than for dense models: {{ pct(1 - W.decode_step.distinct_experts_verify/384) }} of each layer's experts sit idle in SRAM at batch 1.

### Model-specific silicon (Etched-style) vs a decode-specialized programmable ASIC

DeepSeek shipped V3, V3.2, V4 and V4.1 within about two years. Each changed attention (MLA → DSA
indexer → CSA/HCA → CSA2 with cross-layer reuse) and the KV format. A chip that hard-wires V4.1's
dataflow risks obsolescence before volume. What has been stable across those generations, and across
other open MoEs, is narrower:

1. very low-batch decode of fine-grained MoE,
2. FP8/FP4 block-scaled weights,
3. top-k selection (routing and indexing), and
4. tiny-message collectives.

MDA-1 hard-wires only those primitives; everything model-specific is a compiled static schedule.

### ASIC (MDA-1): what silicon adds on top of the software counterfactual

At iso-bandwidth, MDA-1's batch-1 speed is the same as good GPU software. Silicon adds four things:

1. **Cost per unit of HBM bandwidth.** MDA-1 is two {{ D.comparison.asic_area_mm2.die_mm2|n(0) }} mm² dies (${{ D.comparison.asic_unit_cost.die|n(0) }} of silicon) around the same 8 stacks, priced at manufacturing cost plus NRE. Against GPUs bought at $45K and running the same software, that is {{ K.usd_vs_sw|x }} lower $/M tokens. Most of the gap is the GPU's purchase price (vendor margin and compute silicon this workload barely uses), so at $25K/GPU MDA-1 loses ({{ K.usd_vs_sw_lo|x(2) }}).
2. **Energy.** {{ K.tpj_vs_sw|x }} tokens/J against the same-speed GPU if the GPU draws 60% of TDP (range {{ K.tpj_vs_sw_lo|x }}-{{ K.tpj_vs_sw_hi|x }} for 40-100%). We have not measured GPU power, so this is the softest claim here.
3. **Latency by construction.** The static schedule *is* the dataflow execution model, so MDA-1 does not depend on a heroic runtime. Its shorter on-chip hand-off matters little at 4 chips ({{ sp(K.asic_vs_sw - 1) }}).
4. **Capacity right-sizing.** Engram's {{ (W.engram.table_bytes/1e9)|n(0) }} GB of tables need {{ W.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step|si('B') }} per step, so they go to LPDDR. Weights then fit in 8-Hi stacks (192 GB), not 12-Hi.

| decision | choice | reason (number from this study) |
|---|---|---|
| target phase | **decode only**; prefill stays on GPUs | prefill is compute-bound (01 §4); KV hand-off is 890 B/token ({{ (P.ttft.scenarios[1].kv_handoff_ms)|n(2) }} ms for a 4K-token turn) |
| package | **2 dies x 4 HBM3E stacks**, die-to-die link (like B300) | an HBM3E cube is ~11 mm wide; 8 stacks cannot sit on one sub-reticle die |
| memory | 8 x HBM3E, 8 TB/s per package (= GB300) | iso-bandwidth isolates architecture from memory technology |
| compute | 2 PF FP8 / 4 PF FP4xFP8 per package (40% of B300) | batch-1 needs ~0.2 PF; 2 PF keeps the batch-64 frontier near the GPU's |
| matrix engine | 16 rows x wide N, weight-streaming | SCALE-Sim: a 128x128 array reaches 0.3-1.5% utilization on decode GEMVs |
| SRAM | 256 MiB (≥8 MB staging needed at batch 1) | dies are HBM-shoreline-bound with {{ D.comparison.asic_area_mm2.whitespace|n(0) }} mm² of whitespace, so SRAM is nearly free |
| process | TSMC N4P (N5 family), not N3E | SRAM bitcell is 0.021 µm² on both; shoreline-bound dies; N3 wafers ~20% dearer |
| collectives | in-fabric all-reduce, direct 4-package mesh | latency set to the best measured 4-GPU all-reduce (2.37 µs) + 0.2 µs die crossing; no link advantage assumed |

## What a seasoned accelerator architect would weigh

* **The software counterfactual dominates the decision.** If Baseten can ship the dataflow-style runtime, MDA-1's speed is available on hardware they already run, with no new silicon. Build silicon only for $/token and W/token at scale.
* **NRE amortization dominates ASIC cost.** At $300M NRE and 20K packages, NRE is ${{ D.comparison.asic_unit_cost.nre_share|n }} of ${{ D.comparison.asic_unit_cost.total|n }} per package. Break-even against GPUs running the same software at $45K/GPU is about {{ K.be_sw_300|n(-2) }} packages ($300M NRE) or {{ K.be_sw_600|n(-2) }} ($600M). At hyperscaler volume that is easy; for a single inference provider it is the crux.
* **Time to market.** Our assumption: 24-30 months to volume for an N4P two-die package with HBM3E (team-dependent; not sourced). Rubin ships now. Rubin with dataflow-style software projects {{ C.rubin_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user; MDA-1-HBM4 at the same bandwidth, {{ C.asic_hbm4.tok_s_user|n }}.
* **Risk register.** HBM and CoWoS supply (shared with GPU vendors); a static-schedule compiler that keeps up with a new model each quarter; the collective-latency target; demand that pays for low-latency tokens. If the market prices only throughput, GPUs with the same software are {{ (K.b256_sw.tok_s_system/K.b256_asic.tok_s_system)|x(2) }} ahead of MDA-1 per 4 chips at batch 256. MDA-1 carries 40% of their FLOPs.
